A||||1||kslish||2.8L->3.1L rebuild - bore the block?||||1||
Z||000000||kslish||03-06-2001||10:55 PM||||When rebuilding an engine, how common is it to get away with not boring out the block?  <P>The reason I am asking is because I just picked up a set of brand new standard bore Silvolite #1486 pistons for around $5 a piston.  <P>The pistons were such a good deal, I couldn't pass them up, but I'm having some doubts if I'll ever get to use them.  When I did a search on 3.1L stroker rebuilds, it seems like almost everyone bored their block at least 0.020 over.<P>My GT currently has around 104,000 miles on it's 2.8L engine and it has seen better days, which is why I'm lining up the parts for a 3.1L stroker rebuild.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||billyboy||03-06-2001||11:56 PM||||Putting in a stroker kit without boring the block is like waxing your car without washing it first.  If you have new pistons, you'll need to take them to the machine shop along with the rings (that came with the pistons, hopefully) and they will actually bore the cylinders according to the piston rings.  When I did the build up on my MR2, I took the Wiseco pistons and rings in to the machine shop, and they actually bored each individual cylinder with reference to one piston.  When I got everything back in its nice plastic bag, the pistons were labeled on the top for which cylinder to use them in.  Any decent performance machine shop will always tell you to bore the block when you put new pistons in.  It makes for a much better build up, and it'll make the engine pretty much bullet proof.  <P>Also, with that many miles on your motor, you've probably got an egg shape in your cylinders, which just about every car will do with that many miles on it.  Have the machine shop use a torque block on your engine when they machine the cylinders too, if they have one, which they should because 2.8's are a dime a dozen (S-10's, Fiero's, and God knows how many other GM cars) and I'm sure they get rebuilt often.  A torque block bolts down on top of the motor where the heads would bolt on, and it's basically distorting the cylinders in the same way that the heads will when they're put on.  They won't distort much, but enough that doing it will add an extreme amount of life to your motor.<P>Of course you can go without boring the block before you put the pistons in, but let me tell you something... it only cost me $200 to get my block hot tanked, bored, and my heads ported, polished, 3 angle valve job, new valve seats and seals, and the cams re-shimmed.  It's worth ten times the amount you'll pay to have it done, believe me.  When you rebuild a motor and start it up for the first time, and you pinch the EGR hoses off and take off the oil cap and no blow-by comes out, then you know that you have a rock solid motor.  Mine never had an ounce of blow-by, and with over 20k miles, the oil is still amber when I change it.<P>There's no replacement for a well machined and bored block.||||reg||1
